    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-3231",
      "problem": "Find the $x$-intercepts of the circle $(x + 3)^2 + (y - 2)^2 = 4.$",
      "worked_solution": "We must have $y = 0$ at the $x$-intercepts. \n\nSo, we need to substitute $y = 0$ into the equation of the circle and solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ (x+3)^{2}+(0-2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ (x+3)^{2}+4 & =4 \\\\ (x+3)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x+3 & =0 \\\\ x & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercept occurs at the point $(-3,0)$, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14133180/assets/6242b2af154ffea71d523bea97cb44888077b1634f6dd3d2ee05da4feb8e3f5b.png)",
